---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const sagCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '9 - B*(exp(-0.04*x) - exp(-0.2*x))', label: 'dissolved oxygen downstream', color: '#1d4ed8', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '5 + 0*x', label: '≈5 mg/L: many fish stressed', color: '#b42318', width: 1 },
  ],
  params: [
    { name: 'B', label: 'pollution load', min: 2, max: 14, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'mg/L deficit' },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 60, label: 'distance downstream from outfall (km)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 10, label: 'dissolved oxygen (mg/L)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const timelineCfg = {
  unit: 'year',
  events: [
    { year: 1962, title: 'Silent Spring', text: 'Rachel Carson\'s book documents the damage pesticides cause through food chains — often credited with launching the modern environmental movement.', tag: 'pollution' },
    { year: 1972, title: 'Stockholm Conference', text: 'The first UN conference on the human environment; the UN Environment Programme (UNEP) is founded the same year.', tag: 'pollution' },
    { year: 1973, title: 'CITES signed', text: 'The Convention on International Trade in Endangered Species regulates cross-border trade in wild animals and plants.', tag: 'biodiversity' },
    { year: 1985, title: 'Antarctic ozone hole reported', text: 'Farman, Gardiner and Shanklin publish measurements of severe springtime ozone depletion over Antarctica in Nature.', tag: 'pollution' },
    { year: 1987, title: 'Montreal Protocol', text: 'Nations agree to phase out CFCs — the pollutants behind ozone loss; eventually ratified by every UN member state.', tag: 'pollution' },
    { year: 1992, title: 'Rio Earth Summit', text: 'The Convention on Biological Diversity opens for signature at Rio; conservation gets its first global treaty framework.', tag: 'biodiversity' },
    { year: 2015, title: 'Paris Agreement and SDGs', text: '195 states adopt the Paris Agreement on climate change; the UN Sustainable Development Goals are adopted the same year.', tag: 'climate' },
    { year: 2022, title: 'Kunming–Montreal framework', text: 'Nations adopt the Global Biodiversity Framework, including the "30 by 30" goal of protecting 30% of land and sea by 2030.', tag: 'biodiversity' },
  ],
};

const yieldCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'r', label: 'population growth rate', min: 0.1, max: 1.5, step: 0.1, value: 0.6, unit: '/yr' },
    { name: 'K', label: 'carrying capacity', min: 5000, max: 50000, step: 5000, value: 20000, unit: 'tonnes' },
    { name: 'f', label: 'stock as fraction of K', min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.05, value: 0.5, unit: '' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'stock now', expr: 'f*K', unit: 'tonnes' },
    { label: 'population growth this year = max sustainable catch', expr: 'r*f*K*(1-f)', unit: 'tonnes/yr' },
    { label: 'maximum sustainable yield (at stock = K/2)', expr: 'r*K/4', unit: 'tonnes/yr' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/advanced/human-influences-on-ecosystems">
  <h2>The idea: one species remakes the biosphere</h2>
  <p>
    No organism has ever rearranged the planet the way <em>Homo sapiens</em> has — converting
    forests to fields, doubling the nitrogen cycle, moving species between continents. Unit 20
    of the 0610 syllabus is the accounting exercise for that transformation: how we boosted
    <strong>food supply</strong> (20.1), what <strong>habitat destruction</strong> costs
    (20.2), which <strong>pollutants</strong> we add (20.3), and how <strong>conservation</strong>
    tries to keep what remains (20.4). Every section pairs a benefit with a bill; the
    advanced-level skill is explaining the bill in biology.
  </p>

  <h2>Feeding eight billion (20.1)</h2>
  <p>
    Food production rose because we engineered every link: <strong>agricultural machinery</strong>
    to farm larger areas efficiently, <strong>chemical fertilisers</strong> to replace
    <em>nitrate</em> and other minerals, <strong>insecticides</strong> and
    <strong>herbicides</strong> to cut the losses pests and weeds take, and
    <strong>selective breeding</strong> to raise yields of crops and livestock (Core list, all
    five). The costs show up in two systems the syllabus asks you to weigh:
    <strong>monocultures</strong> — vast single-crop fields — are efficient to harvest but
    strip the soil of specific minerals, encourage pest build-up and reduce biodiversity;
    <strong>intensive livestock</strong> production maximises output per animal but raises
    welfare, disease-spread and waste-pollution concerns.
  </p>

  <h2>Bulldozing habitats (20.2)</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Biodiversity</strong> is simply the number of different species in an area — and it
    falls when habitat goes: land cleared for housing, crops and livestock, resources
    extracted, fresh and marine waters polluted (Core reasons). <strong>Deforestation</strong>
    is the case study to explain (Supplement): removing forest <strong>reduces biodiversity
    and causes extinctions</strong> (specialists lose their only habitat), <strong>exposes
    soil</strong> that then erodes, <strong>increases flooding</strong> (no canopy to slow
    rainfall, no roots to hold water), and <strong>raises atmospheric CO₂</strong> — the trees
    are no longer photosynthesising, and often they are burned. Every food web those trees
    supported simplifies from the bottom up.
  </p>

  <h2>Pollution: the oxygen sag (20.3)</h2>
  <p>
    The clearest cause-and-effect chain in ecology is <strong>eutrophication</strong>
    (Supplement): <strong>untreated sewage or excess fertiliser</strong> washes
    <strong>nitrate</strong> and other ions into water → <strong>producers</strong> (algae)
    grow explosively → when they die, <strong>decomposers</strong> multiply, using
    <strong>aerobic respiration</strong> → <strong>dissolved oxygen falls</strong> →
    <strong>fish and other oxygen-demanders die</strong>. The result downstream is the classic
    <strong>oxygen sag curve</strong>: oxygen crashes after the outfall, then recovers as the
    organic load is consumed and re-aeration wins back:
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dissolved oxygen against distance from a sewage outfall — the deeper the load, the deeper the sag (saturation ≈9 mg/L in cool water)" widget="plotter" config={sagCfg} />

  <p>
    Two more pollutants complete 20.3. <strong>Non-biodegradable plastics</strong> persist in
    aquatic and terrestrial habitats: they fragment into microplastics, are ingested by
    animals, smother soil and — floating in ocean gyres — entangle and choke marine life, all
    while never rotting away. <strong>Methane and carbon dioxide</strong> are greenhouse
    gases: added faster than the carbon cycle absorbs them, they strengthen the
    <strong>enhanced greenhouse effect</strong> and drive <strong>climate change</strong>,
    shifting rainfall, melting ice and pushing species that cannot move fast enough toward
    extinction.
  </p>

  <h2>Conservation: using without using up (20.4)</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>sustainable resource</strong> is one produced as fast as it is removed, so it
    never runs out — the definition at the heart of Core point 1. Species become
    <strong>endangered or extinct</strong> through climate change, habitat destruction,
    hunting, overharvesting, pollution and <strong>introduced species</strong> that outcompete
    natives (Core point 3). The remedies are practical: monitoring and protecting species and
    habitats, education, <strong>captive breeding programmes</strong>, and
    <strong>seed banks</strong> (Core point 4) — with Supplement students adding artificial
    insemination and IVF to captive breeding, and the genetic warning that small populations
    lose variation, making them fragile against disease and change. The policy milestones
    form a real timeline:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="sixty years of environmental policy — scrub through it (all dates verifiable)" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <p>
    Fish stocks show the arithmetic best. A population growing logistically gains biomass
    fastest at <strong>half its carrying capacity</strong> — so the smartest fishery keeps the
    stock near K/2 and harvests exactly that growth, forever. Harvest more than the growth and
    the stock shrinks; keep at it and the fishery collapses, which is what quotas, closed
    seasons, protected areas, net mesh sizes and monitoring (Supplement, point 6) are all
    designed to prevent. Play with the model:
  </p>

  <Widget no="3" caption="logistic growth — the catch you can take forever equals the growth you leave alive" widget="sliderLab" config={yieldCfg} />

  <p>
    Why bother at all? The syllabus ends with the reasons (Supplement, point 7):
    <strong>maintaining biodiversity</strong>, <strong>reducing extinction</strong>,
    <strong>protecting vulnerable ecosystems</strong>, and keeping ecosystem functions we
    depend on — <strong>nutrient cycling</strong> and <strong>resource provision</strong>:
    food, drugs (many first found in wild plants and fungi), fuel, and genes we have not yet
    needed. Extinction forecloses options permanently; conservation is the refusal to sign
    that receipt.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Biology 0610 syllabus (2026–2028), unit 20; OpenStax Biology 2e, ch. 47 (Conservation Biology and Biodiversity); UN treaties and milestones (CITES 1973; Montreal Protocol 1987; CBD 1992; Paris Agreement 2015; Kunming–Montreal GBF 2022).</p>
</TopicPage>
